    "result_text": "A claim now belongs to the period containing its due-by date instead of its date, and all four acceptance tests pass with `bin/e2e`.\n\nNo database change was needed: each claim already stores its due-by, set when it is added, and edits, re-dates and moves to another person leave it alone.\n\n**Server**\n- **Adding a claim** (`ClaimService.add`): the due-by is now worked out first (date plus the terms it would get). The add is refused if that day falls in a closed period, and marked a late entry if it falls in a soft-closed one.\n- **Closed-period checks**: edit, opening the editor, open/settle/void, approve, reject, adding a proof, re-settle, defer, re-date, move to another person and split all check the claim's due-by rather than its date. Edit and re-date no longer look at the new date at all. So a claim dated in a closed period with its due-by outside it can still change, and a split is judged by the original claim alone.\n- **Numbering**: per-period numbers follow the due-by. Because the due-by never changes, re-dating no longer renumbers a claim, so I removed the now-unused `SheetService.redatedClaimNumber`.\n- **Split claims**: the new claim is numbered by its own due-by. It is now marked a late entry if its due-by falls in a soft-closed period. That behaviour is new; the scenario 3 test requires it. No `LATE_ENTRY` audit line is written for a split, which matches what the test expects.\n- **Period figures**: the totals a period records when it is closed or soft-closed (`Rollup.dueBetweenByPerson`) now count claims by due-by.\n\n**Front end**\n- The period shown on a claim, and the live period totals per period and per person, now use the claim's due-by.\n\nI also updated the related doc comments.",
